你好

## Current Turn
Turn: tsk_turn; attempt: tsk_turn; input seq (0, 2].

## Current Chat Attachment Delivery
Chat supports attachment delivery through \`remi message send --attachment <path>\`; repeat \`--attachment\` for multiple files. The current conversation identifies the destination; no Feishu chat ID is needed.
- Attach files the user explicitly requested, reports longer than one screen, and charts. Send plain-text conclusions without attachments when no file is needed.
- Do not attach repository source code, raw logs, configuration containing secrets, or files larger than 20MB. The server validates allowed file types and size before queueing delivery.
- For HTML, produce a self-contained file with inline CSS and JavaScript and no external stylesheet, script, or font URLs; do not depend on localStorage, cookies, or parent-frame access.
- Report the attachment IDs and filenames returned by the command. A workspace path alone is not a delivered attachment.
- Incoming Chat attachments are listed with local paths when available. Read those files directly; otherwise use the listed \`remi attachment download\` command. Use lark-cli download only as a fallback when platform attachment access is unavailable.

## Remi Context
Use \`remi context\` for the current identity and allowed operations. Use \`remi project list|get|search\` and \`remi repo list|get|search\` to inspect the database-backed safe directory.
Repositories are not fetched for Chat startup, and \`remi repo list\` never contacts Git. Run \`remi repo checkout <repo-id>\` only when repository files are needed; checkout fetches that one repository and returns timeout or fetch failures as a tool error.

## Current Session Context
这是全新会话，没有先前的会话记忆；从第 0 条开始，最新是第 2 条，共 1 条还没读。
动手前先读完未读的部分，了解上下文：remi message list chat_1 --from 0 --to 2

## Output
When finished, summarize what changed, how it was verified, and any remaining risks."
`;

exports[`plain turn input drops the Issue range read when the triggering comment is complete 1`] = `
"# Bootstrap Prompt

## Current Request
[1 · member mem_alice · request]
please do X

## Current Turn
Turn: tsk_turn; attempt: tsk_turn; input seq (0, 2].

## Issue Workspace Session History
Provider-native historical JSONL for this Issue workspace is available read-only under \`./.multiremi/sessions/\`. Inspect relevant sibling histories when the current task needs their evidence, but do not modify historical files.

## Sharing Results Across Sessions
Historical transcripts are supporting evidence, while published Session results are the canonical cross-session handoff. If you produce a durable decision, artifact, or finding that other Sessions should reuse, explicitly publish only that result. Do not republish an unchanged result.
Use a quoted HEREDOC so the shell cannot rewrite the result:

    cat <<'RESULT' | remi session result publish iss_1 --session ises_1 --title "Short title" --type decision --content-stdin
    Reusable result only; omit private working notes.
    RESULT
Tag the result with \`--type mr|report|deploy|decision|doc|other\` so it is filed under the right icon, and link what it points at with repeatable \`--ref issue:<id>\` / \`--ref task:<id>\` / \`--ref url:https://…\` (a merge request, a document, a task).

## Issue
Key: MUL-1
Title: Plain input

## Triggering Comment
Alice just left a new comment. Focus on this comment and do not confuse it with previous comments.
The comment body appears once in Current Request; use it as the authoritative text.

If you decide to reply, post it as a comment. Always use the trigger comment ID below, and do not reuse --parent values from previous turns.

Use --content-stdin with a quoted HEREDOC so the shell cannot rewrite backticks, $(), variables, quotes, or formatting:

    cat <<'COMMENT' | remi message send ises_1 --kind reply --reply-to msg_1 --content-stdin
    First paragraph.

    Second paragraph.
    COMMENT

## Creating Follow-up Issues
Pick the target project explicitly before creating an issue: \`remi project list\` lists every project (\`--output json\` includes each project's \`default_assignee_type\`/\`default_assignee_id\`), and \`remi project defaults <project>\` prints one project's default assignee. Then run \`remi issue create --title <title> --project <id> --use-project-defaults\` to route the new issue to that project's default assignee.

## Shared Workspace Coordination
Other Agents may run concurrently in the same repository checkouts. Your execution directory contains private task configuration, not a separate code checkout. Use the reported repository paths, coordinate overlapping edits, preserve others' changes, and never switch the shared branch. Do not assume another Agent has finished just because one task completed.

## Issue Execution and Escalation
Read \`remi issue responsibility iss_1 --output json\` for the execution owner chain and unavailable reasons. An Issue's execution owner is its assigned Agent or the assigned team's available Leader, never the first team of the message sender. A missing or archived Leader is a blocker; do not substitute another member.
Delegated work reports return to the actual delegator's original Session/scope, including cross-team delegation. They do not transfer Issue ownership.
As this Issue's execution owner, your final reply of the round is its summary; when the round ends the Issue moves to \`in_review\` automatically. Never set an Issue you execute to \`done\`.
As a parent Issue's execution owner, check each child's result. If it is right, run \`remi issue status <child> done\`. If it needs changes, find the child's Issue Session with \`remi session list <child> --output json\` and send \`remi message send <child-session-id> --to issue-owner --kind request --content "<what to change>"\`.
Ask questions only through native AskUserQuestion, which creates one original Q; \`remi message send --kind decision\` is an ordinary conversation choice and creates no Q. Every Q, permission requests included, climbs the execution owner chain: this Issue, then each parent up to the root, and above the root the workspace members. For a Q routed to you, read it with \`remi message question get <Q> --output json\`. Answer with \`remi message question answer <Q> --revision <route_revision> --data '{"response":{"selected_options":["<options[].value>"]}}'\` (for a permission request, the chosen option's value is its option_id) or \`--data '{"response":{"answers":{"<question>":"<answer>"}}}'\`. If you cannot decide, use \`remi message question escalate <Q> --revision <route_revision> --reason <reason>\`. Do not create a second AUQ in the parent or Remi Session. Consult a Senior through ordinary directed collaboration messages.
Remi presents context and recommendations through \`remi message question present <Q> --revision <route_revision> --summary <summary>\` while preserving the original questions and options. Answer saved is not proof the original call resumed: report wait_status and actual consumption. An explicit answer revision uses --revise --answer-revision <answer_revision> --reason <reason>, preserving history without replaying the old call.

## Output
When finished, summarize what changed, how it was verified, and any remaining risks."
`;

